From: Will Cooke <will.cooke@chainguard.dev>
Subject: [PATCH] ncrypto: always pass pkcs5=1 to EVP_KDF PBKDF2

Node 24.22.0 and 26.x derive PBKDF2 keys through EVP_KDF and only set
OSSL_KDF_PARAM_PKCS5=1 when built against OpenSSL < 4, to match
PKCS5_PBKDF2_HMAC(). Chainguard's openssl-4.0 reverts upstream
66ab2db185ac, so PKCS5_PBKDF2_HMAC() still disables the SP 800-132
lower-bound checks as in 3.x. Pass pkcs5=1 unconditionally so Node keeps
matching it: non-approved PBKDF2 parameters remain usable under the FIPS
provider (flagged by the indicator) instead of failing with
"invalid key length".

Same approach as the Ruby openssl gem pkcs5 default
(ruby/openssl e87750e2).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/deps/ncrypto/ncrypto.cc
+++ b/deps/ncrypto/ncrypto.cc
@@ -2860,11 +2860,10 @@
   const char* md_name = EVP_MD_get0_name(md);
   if (md_name == nullptr) return {};
 
-#if OPENSSL_VERSION_MAJOR < 4
-  // Match PKCS5_PBKDF2_HMAC: OpenSSL 3 disables the provider's lower bounds,
-  // while OpenSSL 4 leaves them at their provider defaults.
+  // Match PKCS5_PBKDF2_HMAC: disable the provider's SP 800-132 lower bounds.
+  // Chainguard's OpenSSL 4 keeps the 3.x PKCS5_PBKDF2_HMAC behaviour, so do
+  // the same here regardless of the OpenSSL major version.
   int pkcs5 = 1;
-#endif
   int iteration_count = static_cast<int>(iterations);
   unsigned char empty_salt = 0;
   OSSL_PARAM params[] = {
@@ -2872,9 +2871,7 @@
         OSSL_KDF_PARAM_PASSWORD,
         const_cast<char*>(pass.data == nullptr ? "" : pass.data),
         pass.data == nullptr ? 0 : pass.len),
-#if OPENSSL_VERSION_MAJOR < 4
     OSSL_PARAM_construct_int(OSSL_KDF_PARAM_PKCS5, &pkcs5),
-#endif
     OSSL_PARAM_construct_octet_string(
         OSSL_KDF_PARAM_SALT,
         salt.data == nullptr && salt.len == 0
